ls and dual horizon weights. Run it on unequal-low droughts, repeated diluted droughts, alternating two-level prefixes with varying ell/h, and small three-level paths. Ask explorers to extract human-readable certificates from surprising outputs.","title":"Finite-Prefix LP Laboratory","why_promising":"The current bottlenecks are structural guesses about which horizons bind. Exact small LP certificates can quickly falsify proposed recursions and suggest the correct active-set or pooling rule."},{"id":"idea_07","risks":"The construction may only restate already known rare-event caveats unless it yields clean all-horizon expected-regret bounds.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample construction","probability-weighted lower bounds"],"summary":"Use equal-low or unequal-low drought blocks as rare branches in a stochastic exogenous process: conditional on a rare event, insert a long block with large local D_n, but choose probabilities so p_i N_i=o(T_i). Compute whether essential-sup or post-history information-tail quantities stay large while pseudo-regret remains o(T).","title":"Rare Hard Drought Branches","why_promising":"This would stress-test any future information-tail necessity condition and sharpen the existing rare-history obstruction using the strongest current finite-prefix gadgets."}],"solution_status":"unsolved"}
