./logs/iter_017_ideas.stderr.log:5137:{"bibliography_candidates":[{"authors":"Awi Federgruen and Henri Groenevelt","relevance":"Useful proof-technology background for cumulative/nested resource-allocation constraints and greedy optimality, potentially relevant to front-loaded low-mass constructions in multi-low drought LPs.","title":"The Greedy Procedure for Resource Allocation Problems: Necessary and Sufficient Conditions for Optimality","url":"https://business.columbia.edu/sites/default/files-efs/pubfiles/4071/federgruen_greedy_procedure.pdf","venue_year":"Operations Research, 1986"},{"authors":"Miriam Ayer, H. D. Brunk, G. M. Ewing, W. T. Reid, and Edward Silverman","relevance":"Classical source for pool-adjacent-violators/isotonic grouping; may be relevant if unequal-low drought reductions become a monotone pooling/front-loading problem.","title":"An Empirical Distribution Function for Sampling with Incomplete Information","url":"https://projecteuclid.org/journals/annals-of-mathematical-statistics/volume-26/issue-4/An-Empirical-Distribution-Function-for-Sampling-with-Incomplete-Information/10.1214/aoms/1177728423.full","venue_year":"Annals of Mathematical Statistics, 1955"}],"ideas":[{"id":"idea_01","risks":"The dominance reduction may hide a boundary case: quotes at low times, M=0, zero S_j, or post-low coordinates might create a horizon constraint not represented by the cumulative x_i variables.","suggested_lenses":["proof audit","edge-case verification"],"summary":"Audit the solver's equal-low multi-low drought proof by deriving the full finite-prefix primal LP for h^M,0^{N_0},ell,0^{N_1},ell,...,0^{N_r},ell,0^L,h and explicitly eliminating dominated actions and dominated horizons. The target is to prove the exact reduction to variables x_i with constraints z >= S_j - ell sum_{i<=j} x_i and z >= (h-ell) sum_i x_i, including all capacities and strict-crossing left-limit conventions.","title":"Promote Equal-Low Drought Formula","why_promising":"This is the most immediate partial theorem from the solver attempt, and it would turn CL-048 from candidate into an accepted reusable regression test for the two-level gluing program."},{"id":"idea_02","risks":"Unequal lows may be too broad for a closed form; small examples may show several active horizons and no simple last-low formula.","suggested_lenses":["finite-dimensional LP derivation","counterexample search"],"summary":"Study a single drought with unequal lows, h^M,0^{N_0},ell_1,0^{N_1},ell_2,...,0^{N_r},ell_r,0^L,h. First restrict to two or three distinct low levels and derive the reduced action menu {ell_j^-, h^-}. Look for a PAVA-like pooling rule or for a minimal counterexample where an earlier low horizon binds even though the last low has the largest raw surplus.","title":"Unequal-Low Droughts","why_promising":"The equal-low proof's monotonicity is the fragile point; unequal lows are the next place genuine front-loading constraints should appear if the two-level recursion has real structure."},{"id":"idea_03","risks":"Repeated droughts may be easy because later quote opportunities dilute or repair earlier debt; a naive additive lower bound is likely false.","suggested_lenses":["surplus-debt recursion","LP experiments"],"summary":"Analyze paths made of several low droughts separated by highs, such as h^{M_0},0^{N_1},ell,0^{L_1},h^{K_1},0^{N_2},ell,0^{L_2},h. Track how low-quote mass used to repair a low horizon creates persistent high debt at all later high-dominated horizons, and test whether the state can be summarized by a reflected surplus/debt variable.","title":"Repeated-Drought Debt Recursion","why_promising":"The global D_n=o(n) gluing question likely turns on whether many diluted local conflicts can accumulate through shared high debt; equal-low single-drought formulas provide a clean base case."},{"id":"idea_04","risks":"A boundary-augmented certificate may become tautological or fail to imply actual D_n mass at comparable scales.","suggested_lenses":["dual c